# The foreign-only rescue licence lives at the WORD. Ruled 2026-08-29 and
# kept whole by the 2026-09-15 ruling, which moved the test off the arm: an
# `(err _)` arm matches a bare err anywhere now, and the one place provenance
# is still asked is `rescue`. A `.?` written in the package that raised the
# failure hands it on without entering its callback; the same `.?` on a
# failure raised elsewhere enters it.
#
# The callback is a lambda on purpose. A lambda has no arms to decline with,
# so if the word entered it on an own failure the first line would read
# "claimed mine" — which is what every engine printed before the licence
# moved, and what this pins against.
#
# Both shapes of subject: a bare err decided at the word, and a boxed one
# decided when the chain runs. `when_failed` is std/testing's, foreign to
# this file, so it may read the bare failure the word handed on. The boxed
# own failure has nobody foreign left to read it and ends the program at the
# endpoint, which is a report on stderr and an exit of 1, so that shape is
# pinned in the runtime corpus: a_rescue_on_its_own_boxed_failure_reaches_
# the_endpoint.
import "std/json"
import "std/os"
import "std/testing"

fn mine _
  err "mine"

fn still r
  "still failed, {r}"

own = mine 1 .? (e -> "claimed {e.reason}")

far = json/decode "[1, 2" .? (e -> "claimed {e.reason}")

# The boxed reason is not printed: the playground has no filesystem and
# says so in its own words, and what this line pins is that a foreign
# boxed failure enters the callback at all.
far_boxed = os/read_file! "no_such_file" .? (_ -> "claimed")

pub play =
  print "own: {testing/when_failed own still}"
  >> print "far: {far}"
  >> far_boxed .> (v -> print "far boxed: {v}")
